Ditto.

> You aren't going to find a better platform than a Sun when you
> consider performance and cost.  At the very high end I suppose
> an Origin 2000 (SGI) might outperform a loaded E10000 but I doubt the
> cost difference would justify it.  Certainly clustered E10000s
> are going to do just as well.

I'd put an E10000 up against an Origin 2000 any day of the week. With the CrayLink, SGI solved
one of the problems of the "LAN in a can" NUMA clusters - bandwidth between CPU systems -
but they can't do much about the latency, which means that the scalability drops off significantly
faster than linear as you add CPUs. The E1000 scales at 92% of linear from 4 to 64 CPUs, and
does even better than that up to 48.

> BTW, what kind of "MIP" are you talking about with the Sysplex -
> an IBM Meaningless Indicator of Performance?  I forget how this
> compares to a "VAX Mip" (VAX Meaningless..) - if memory serves
> it is a LOT less.  Anyway, a 64 processor E10000 is going to be
> around 25,000 VAX mips.  Care to compare the price of the Sysplex
> to the E10000?  Care to compare the hoards of droids it takes to
> keep a Sysplex operating?

I suspect he's talking IBM MIPS. In those, the E10000 comes in at about twice the Hitachi
Skyline, which is itself a good bit faster than anything IBM is putting out now. The E10000
also has significantly more I/O bandwidth and aggregate throughput than the Hitachi.

> I guess there are still lots of people with blue pinstripe glazing
> on their glasses :-)

Yup. IBM stock is back up over $100. :-)


