In article <349ED849.3E39@atcon.com>,
	John Allen <johna@atcon.com> writes:
> Hi:
> 
> Simple question.
> 
> I usually am logged onto my AlphaStation running 
> Digital Unix 3.2 as a non-root user.
> 
> Every so often, I would like to su to root to do
> some sysadmin type stuff.  Presently, I can only
> log onto root on my console.
> 
> How do I allow myself (the non-root user) to su to
> root?
> 
> John A. - GeoNet Technolgies Inc.

Dear John,

	first be sure that the file /etc/securettys exists and that it
contains the following lines:
/dev/console
local:0
:0

You might add pttys as well, see 'man securettys'.

'man su' show that you also have to put your username to the group '0'
(i.e. system) in /etc/group.

Good luck.

						Yours, Hans.
