[ Knut Langsetmo ]

>>  1.  how do we set it up to recognize another machine on our network
>>  that is our domain name server?  our internet configuration is set up,
>>  and works if you put in the ip addresses.  i couldn't see where to
>>  put in the ip address for the domain name server.

You need to ...

  --  Make sure /etc/resolv.conf has a "nameserver" entry with the
      IP address of your DNS server (and, quite possibly, a "search"
      entry as well).

  --  In /etc/svc.conf, change the "hosts=local" to "hosts=bind,local"
      (or "hosts=local,bind", if that's what you prefer).

The first of these is pretty standard across all UNIXes; the second is
a feature I didn't encounter before my first exposure to {OSF/1,DUnix}.

>>  2.  how do we enable user login, both at the console and via telnet
>>  clients.  at present, only root can login at the console.  all others,
>>  including users at the console, get the message "too many users", even
>>  if no one else is logged in.

You need to install the appropriate multi-user license PAK(s).  Those would
have come with your system if you purchased them.  If not, you have to buy
them now, or (if your institution participates in CSLG) get them from your
local CSLG administrator.

