You should try using the ANSI modes of the default compiler (DEC C)
to compile code with preprocessor directives not in column 1. You
may use either -std (relaxed ANSI) or -std1 (strict ANSI).

That feature, and others are ANSI only and are not supported in the
common (or K&R mode). The following program will demonstate:

% cat t.c
# include <stdio.h>
#define OK 1

void main() {
  #if OK == 1
  printf("Expected result in -std/-std1\n");
  #else
  printf("Unexpected result in -std0\n");
  #endif
}

% cc t.c; a.out
cc: Warning: t.c, line 5: # not in column 1 is ignored, skipping to end
of line.
  #if OK == 1
--^
cc: Warning: t.c, line 7: # not in column 1 is ignored, skipping to end
of line.
  #else
--^
cc: Warning: t.c, line 9: # not in column 1 is ignored, skipping to end
of line.
  #endif
--^
Expected result in -std/-std1
Unexpected result in -std0

% cc t.c -std; a.out
Expected result in -std/-std1

% cc t.c -std1 ; a.out
Expected result in -std/-std1


I think you will find that the default compiler (DEC C) in V4.0B is
much improved over gcc in both features and runtime performance.
See http://www.unix.digital.com/ for more info.

If you have further questions, let me know.

-Jeff
