Andrew Ireland wrote:
> 
> Hi,
> 
> in the /etc/syslog.conf file, entries with the /var/adm/syslog.dated/<log
> file> have new
> log directories and log files created once per day (or when syslogd is
> restarted).
> 
> Is there any way to configure syslog to create a log directory which will
> log  files
> and create new directories say once per month?
> 

There are two lines in the /var/spool/cron/crontabs/root entry that
are pertinent to the syslogd operation.

The following is the line that actually restarts the logging 
process. As you can see, I have chosen midnight to start my logs.
If you want to only do this once a month then just change the '*' in
the 3d field to be the day of the month that you wish to start
your logs on.

0 0 * * * kill -1 `/bin/cat /var/run/syslog.pid`

The line below here is the line that controls how often the old
directories get purged. The key to this is the '-ctime' argument
in the 'find' cmd.

40 4 * * * find /var/adm/syslog.dated -depth -type d -ctime +5 -exec rm
-rf {} \;


Charlie

