Subject: X11R6.3 ColorMap problem
Date: 1997/12/19
Message-ID: <349AD1D2.D30AD061@slscomp.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 308658401
Distribution: inet
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
X-Priority: 3 (Normal)
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Organization: Surf Network
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.windows.x,comp.windows.x.intrinsics,comp.unix.osf.misc,comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.windows.x.motif


Hardware - SPARCStation20 ( 2 ROSS 150MHz CPU's, 4 Turbo GX+ Frame
Buffers )
Xserver - X11R6.3 - no patches - ( process bound to 2nd CPU )
Motif - 1.2.4
Solaris 2.5 - no patches

/usr/X11R6.3/bin/X -auth
/usr/X11R6.3/lib/X11/xdm/authdir/authfiles/A:0-a0003s

xdm - X Display Manager with support for XDMCP

mwm -multiscreen ( 4 displays )

Our application at one point calls XAllocColorCells  to allocate
 read/write color cells. This in order to perform colormap swapping
 for a double buffer mechanism.

It appears that sometimes (randommly) the function XAllocColorCells
 returns the color cell number one (the black one) that is suppose to be

 a read only one reserved by the system. We are using this cell to store

 the color we need for the double buffer mechanism doing a
 XStoreColors to change the colormap entries of the pixel values
returned
 by XAllocColorCells.

But when XAllocColorCells returns the cell number 1 as the first
 writable cell, the Xserver starts to have an impredictable behavior,
 often crashing the XServer.  The black color used by other
applications is also changed ( the title of the xterm for exmaple).

Restarting xdm of course fixes the problem.

Below is a simplified example of what our application does:

void SetColors(Display *display,int screen)
{
register int i;
unsigned long       plane_masks[1];
Colormap   def_cmap;
long       pixel_list[256];
long       pixel_list_len = 0;
XColor     color[1];

def_cmap = DefaultColormap(display, screen);

while(1)
   {
   if(!XAllocColorCells(display,
                        def_cmap,
                        False,
                        plane_masks,
                        0,
                        &(pixel_list[pixel_list_len]), 1))  break;
    printf(" pixel [%d] =
%d",pixel_list_len,pixel_list[pixel_list_len]);
    pixel_list_len++;
    }

printf("\n");
for(i = 0;i<pixel_list_len;i++)
   {
         color[0].red = 0;
         color[0].blue =0 ;
         color[0].green = 0xffff;
         color[0].pixel= pixel_list[i];
         color[0].flags = DoRed | DoGreen | DoBlue;
         XStoreColors(display,def_cmap,color[0],1);
         printf(" pixel_list[%d] = %d \n",i,pixel_list[i]);
  }
XFlush(display);
}


TIA - Steve ( steve@slscomp.com )




