In article <3490064A.38612861@univ-valenciennes.fr> Frederic BROSSEL <Frederic.Brossel@univ-valenciennes.fr> writes:

> when trying to establish a telnet session with a server running Digital
> UNIX V3.2C  (Rev. 148),
> I have the following message :
>
> telnetd : All Network Ports in use
>
> There already are 31 telnet session, but I can't create one more !

How many seats are you licensed for? And do you have enough terminal
devices available to support them?

1) % lmf list
   Check the OSF-USR line. What is the total? You want at least this
   many devices present.

2) % ls /dev/tty[p-z]*
   Check the number of devices found. If less than the number listed
   for OSF-USR, you need to add more terminal devices.

Note: I have found that the OS will occasionally fail to give out ttyp0
      to users, limiting you to 31 logins.  So if you have 32 licensed
      seats and 32 terminals (the default installed by the OS), adding
      one or more sets of terminal devices may solve the problem.

Adding more terminal devices is done as follows:

  % cd /dev
  % ./MAKEDEV pty2            <-- adds 16, total now 48
  % ./MAKEDEV pty3            <-- adds 16, total now 64

Keep going for as many as needed.

Luck,

MarkG
