In article <34950E40.A065520D@pc.jaring.my>, jahan <jahan@pc.jaring.my> writes:

>Would some DU 4 expert shed some "bright light" on  device names ?
>
There are a variety of devices available for each type you list.  I'll
attempt to be complete.

>Ehternet Card =                  raw = [ none ]

DE422 and DE425; EISA cards for the DEC 2000
DEMNA; XMI module for the DEC 7000 and DEC 8400 with XMI
PMAD; TURBOchannel card for the DEC 3000 family
DE205; ISA card for those system with a ISA slots
DE434, DE435, DE450; PCI Ethernet card
DE500; PCI Fast Ethernet card

Ethernet controllers don't have special file entries.  However,
if you want to pick up packets at a low level look at the
documentation for the Packet Filter.

>CDROM         =                  raw = [ /dev/rrz#c ]

RRD42, RRD43, RRD44, RRD45; SCSI CDROMs.  The device name will
always be an "rz" device.  The '#' in the example is the host
unit number found from:

# = (bus-number * 8) + target-id

This is true for all SCSI devices, with the slightly exception
in tapes.

>Harddisk      =                  raw = [ /dev/rrz#?, /dev/rre#? or /dev/rra#? ]

rz = SCSI connected disk.
re = SCSI disk behind a backplane RAID controller (KZ{E,P}SC and KZPAC).
ra = MSCP disk behind a KDM70 or HSC controller.

The ? in the names is the partition letter.

The supported SCSI disks are:

RZ24L, RZ25, RZ25F, RZ25L, RZ25M, RZ26, RZ26F, RZ26L, RZ26N,
RZ28, RZ28B, RZ28D, RZ28M, RZ29B, RZ55, RZ56, RZ57, RZ58,
RZ73 and RZ74 SCSI disks.  Depending on the platform, some
of the newer DS-RZ??? disks may also be supported.

These solid state disks are also supported: EZ31, EZ32, EZ51, RZ54,
EZ58, EZ64, EZ69.

The supported SCSI RAID subsystem are the HSZ10, HSZ40 and HSZ50.
I think patches to V4.0B are available for the HSZ70 based sub-
systems.

The suppored DSA disks are:

RA60, RA71, RA72, RA73, RA81, RA82, RA90, RA92.

(I don't know why the RA70 isn't listed in the SPD).

>Floppy        =                  raw = [ /dev/rds0c ]

RX23, RX26, RX33

>Tape          =                  raw = [ /dev/[n]rmt#{a,l,m,h} ]

DAT: TLZ04, TLZ06, TLZ07, TLZ09
8mm: TKZ08, TKZ09
3480: TKZ60, TA90 (TMSCP), TA91 (TMSCP)
9track: TSZ07, TA78 (TMSCP), TA79 (TMSCP)
DLT: TZ30, TZ85, TZ86, TZ87, TZ88, TZ89
QIC: TZK10 and TZK11

All the previous tapes are SCSI unless otherwise noted.

Tape special file names are assigned sequentially when the
special files are created.  The form of the name is independent
of the underlying architecture.  To create the special files
by hand, use:

# cd /dev
# ./MAKEDEV tz#

where '#' use (bus * 8) + target-id as for SCSI disks.  For
TMSCP tapes use: tms#, where the '#' is the unit number that
you assign to the drive.  The base system have minimal if
any support for medium changers, but the drive in one of
these should be supported as the drive.  Various layered
product provide support for the media changers.

To identify what tape corresponds to what special file, use
the file command:

# file /dev/rmt*h

Most of the information I've given here is documented in the
Software Product Description for the operating system and
the manual pages in Section 7.

