Regrettably, my points are relevant to most of the newsgroups, and I
wish to point out some very serious errors in the previous posting.

>In article <66kl8h$pr7$1@lyra.csx.cam.ac.uk>,
>Nick Maclaren <nmm1@cus.cam.ac.uk> wrote:
>>Don't use GNU tar.  The kindest description of that program is that
>>it got cancerous featuritis some time ago, and should be put down.
>
>Without getting into a blow-by-blow code review of GNU vs other tars, I'd
>like to strongly disagree with Nick's advice.  Nick is right insofar as
>a lot of the GNU tar source code is ugly, really ugly.  But it works,
>and it works well.  IMHO (and I think many knowledgable users would
>share MHOs) GNU tar ranks at the top of the tars:

There are two reasons why not:

    1) If you ever try to port it to a Unix it has never been ported to
before, it is likely to be hell.  I tried, on one system that was a variant
of HP-UX and other that was an OSF/1 derivative, and decided that it was a
waste of time.  So I used Gilmore tar.

    2) There were (and probably still are) some VERY serious security flaws
in GNU tar.  It makes assumptions that all systems work the way it expects;
if they don't, you may well end up with strange ownerships, groups or
permissions (often without a diagnostic), and possibly even worse problems.


