Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.unix.misc,comp.unix.programmer


I've recently had a problem with FlexLM vendor daemons built on our
Digital Unix 4.0B 564 that wouldn't run on various customers' machines
which had *identical* OS revisions and patches (ie none).  Basically the
loader would complain about unresolved symbols that had null names on
the customer machine.

How is this possible unless the OS are indeed quite different for
different hardware platforms even at the library and loader level?
What sort of confidence can we have in any tests we run if this
is true?

It turns out the problem were due to Flex's lmstrip command removing
too many symbols but it still doesn't explain the different behaviour
of the loader under identical OS revisions.

Second gripe:

Why is OSF's make so badly broken? All our makefiles work with the
native make program on every other flavour of Unix I have ever seen.  I
have always had to install GNU make on OSF but now even that doesn't
work right on 4.0B. The main problem is that there's no default rule
for updating a library member from a .c file, i.e., you can't say:

LIB        =   liblib.a
LIBMEMBERS =   $(LIB)(file1.o) $(LIB)(file2.o)

$(LIB): $(LIBMEMBERS)
	$(RANLIB) $(LIB)

and expect $(LIB)(file1.o) to be recompiled if file1.c is newer than
the archive member.  I installed the latest GNU make 3.76.1 BTW and
at the moment I have to `ar dv $(LIB) file.o' to force make to
consider a rebuild when I've update thed .c file.

What is the easiest way to fix this apart from writing rules
for each individual object file?

And while I on a rant, isn't the most stupid default rule the one that
does an `sccs get' of the latest version of a file if the s.file is
newer?  Don't people ever want to build a stable or old version of their
software?

-- 
