In article <MPG.efa25a630b8b0e8989681@mitswa>
cmuir@mitswa.com.au.no.spam.please (Chris Muir) writes:
> rsh -l oracle Bobafett ls
> The user 'oracle' has a .rhosts file located in its home directory.  The 
> entry in the file specifies 'Bobafett root'.
> ... the command reports back 'Permission Denied' and fails ...

Are you using C2 security? Check that user oracle is not marked Nologin in
/etc/passwd. If it is you have to use vipw to allow oracle login: neither
dxaccounts nor edauth will do. Also try (as root) '/bin/login -f oracle':
beware of password expiry.

Also check the rsh setup: that you are running rshd in /etc/inetd.conf; the
permissions on the .rhosts file (read 'man rhosts'), ...

In article <3490ce97.525375862@news.virgin.net> not.me@a.b.com (John
Sheldrake) also replies:
> As well as entries in $HOME/.rhosts you should have entries in
> /etc/hosts.equiv ...

No, you do not need that. Only add things to /etc/hosts.equiv if you are
absolutely sure you want them.
