In article <66otou$mpo@mxgate.memex.co.uk>, Stephen Marley <stephen@bach.memex.co.uk> writes:
>Why is OSF's make so badly broken? All our makefiles work with the
>native make program on every other flavour of Unix I have ever seen.  I
>have always had to install GNU make on OSF but now even that doesn't
>work right on 4.0B. The main problem is that there's no default rule
>for updating a library member from a .c file, i.e., you can't say:
>

I think you'll find that make never really handled libraries properly.
Even if you do have a .a.o rule it's hideously slow because the whole
library is re-written for _each_ .o as it is compiled.  I keep the .o's
hanging around, blow away the library and then use ar q:

    $(LIB)      : $(LIBOBJ)
                @-rm -f $(LIB)
                ar qv $(LIB) $(LIBOBJ)
                $(RANLIB) $(LIB)

