In article <669n60$n1q$1@Starbase.NeoSoft.COM>, Cameron Laird wrote:
>I'm doing administration of a Digital Unix 4.0 host.  The
>organization wants a finger daemon that returns only on
>exact matches (that is, a client who commands "finger bob"
>will see data only on "bob", not "bob_adams", "bob_bell",
>...).  Suggestions, anyone?  I think I might be able to
>munge inetd's invocation of fingerd so that it does this;

This sounds like the correct route.  The fingerd on my system (FreeBSD) has
an option that lets you specify the program that it uses to look up local
information.  If the default one on Digital Unix doesn't, I'm sure that you
can get another one off the net that does.  You should also look at the man
page for finger(1).  Some versions have options that allow you to tell it
only to match against user names, etc.  If finger(1) doesn't support exactly
what you want, just wrap it in a script and have fingerd invoke that.

Something else to consider is that finger(1) doesn't necessarily use the
network for local lookups.  Exactly what happens depends, not surprisingly,
on your local setup and the OS you are using.

