As I understand it there are two modes of configuring swap - lazy and
greedy. Greedy mode decides at process start up exactly how much swap it
needs and reserves it. Lazy mode basically involves processes only going
to get more swap if and when it requires more and so has no need to
reserve any.

The main difference between these two modes is the way that they fall
down if they run out of swap. As greedy is default I assume this is the
safest. When you start to out of swap you get a nice little error
message and the offending process that tried to reserve the space is
allowed to run but if swap is exceeded to process is murdered. In lazy
mode I think things are more catastophic and a possible chain of
processes are killed. I'm pretty sure I was told that this can cause a
system crash. 

So, if you want to avoid a crash and allow your processes to be lazy
ensure that you have plenty of swap. 

