In article <65shua$o4n@antares.utu.fi>, arno@antares.utu.fi (Arno Hahma) writes:

>Can you swap directly on the subdisks or plexes in a disk group?

Does it have a block device interface?  If not, then you can't
swap to it.  As far as I can tell only final volumes and the
original starting devices have block devices associated with
them.

>It
>would be quite a job to extract the swap partitions out of the disk
>groups and use conventional swapping on them. Digital Unix does support
>more than two swap partitions, doesn't it?

Yes it does.

>I thought you could just add
>the plex or subdisk names directly into /etc/fstab. Is it possible?

Which returns to the question of what is the name of a sub-disk or
plex; /dev/?.  Since these components of LSM don't have special
files, they don't offer the necessary block interface.

>According to the documents it is better to use individual swap areas
>rather than a striped or concatenated volume. That is why I'd like to
>use one swap partition on each physical disk (5 altogether) and through
>LSM to enable mirroring later.

I can see not using a concatenated volume since it offers no
bandwidth or through-put advantages over a collection of devices
or a stripe set.  I'm not sure why don't recommend a stripe set.
Depending on the paging and swapping load it could easily become
bandwidth limited and striping may have an advantage there.  The
last I was told space allocation was done round robin and not
as a stripe set (which ULTRIX used).  This would do well distributing
multiple paging I/Os among the devices, but if you one big swap
that tends to be bandwidth limited.

