From: mcbinc@world.std.com (Monty C. Brandenberg)
Subject: Re: Can you lock a kernel device driver to a single processor?
Date: 1997/12/04
Message-ID: <EKnGM4.J4A@world.std.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 295200885
Sender: news@world.std.com (Mr Usenet Himself)
References: <347EC6B3.319DF0B7@uk.oracle.com> 
Reply-To: mcbinc@world.std.com
Organization: MCB, Inc.
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


In <347EC6B3.319DF0B7@uk.oracle.com> Rick Morrell wrote:
> Is it possible to configure a kernel device driver so that its
> associated threads only execute on a single CPU in a multi-processor
> system?
> 

Depends what you mean by 'associated threads.'  If this means user-mode
application threads invoking the driver via the standard cdevsw/bdevsw-
style entry points, then you can declare your driver to be 'funneled' and
it will run only on the master cpu.  If you mean user-mode threads invoking
the driver via other mechanisms, see the unix_master()/unix_release() 
calls.  If you mean mach-style kernel threads, generally, the mach api's are
there for you to use, though they're not always well-documented. 

