>cleaning up.  You lose buffered output because the C run-time system
>is not called to write it.
>
>Most files are closed fairly quickly, though there can be problems
>with NFS, but the problem you are seeing is with closing sockets.
>These are a Berkeleyism, and have been kludged up most horribly on
>all Unices.  One of the effects is that it isn't always possible to
>do an emergency close from either end - you have to wait for the
>other end to close the socket.
>
>Sorry, but all you can do is to moan at your vendor, who will make
>evasive noises.  You MAY get them to implement another kludge, with
>the hope of reducing the frequency of this effect.

